WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:09.166
A live stream starts and five million viewers hit play at once. Without a CDN the origin server collapses.

00:00:10.366 --> 00:00:11.294
Pause and think.

00:00:11.694 --> 00:00:14.005
What is request collapsing at the edge?

00:00:14.405 --> 00:00:21.719
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:28.090
A content delivery network keeps copies of popular files close to the people who watch them.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:30.903
Think of a bakery chain.

00:00:31.353 --> 00:00:35.497
Baking everything in one central factory means long delivery trips.

00:00:35.947 --> 00:00:43.354
So each neighborhood shop keeps the most popular bread on its shelves, and only orders from the factory when it runs out.

00:00:43.804 --> 00:00:47.427
The shops are edge caches, and the factory is the origin.

00:00:47.483 --> 00:00:58.629
New word: cache. A small fast store of recently used data so you don't fetch it from the slow place again. Picture it like this: Keeping the salt next to the stove instead of in the basement.

00:00:59.029 --> 00:01:09.408
New word: edge. A connection between two nodes in a graph; also "the edge" = servers close to users. Picture it like this: A road between two cities.

00:01:11.933 --> 00:01:13.442
Here is a request path.

00:01:13.892 --> 00:01:16.992
A viewer asks the nearest edge for a video chunk.

00:01:17.442 --> 00:01:19.950
On a hit the edge answers immediately.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:25.914
On a miss it asks a regional shield cache, and only then the origin, then keeps a copy.

00:01:27.264 --> 00:01:29.633
Words to use in a design interview.

00:01:30.083 --> 00:01:35.505
Hit ratio, the share of requests served from cache, drives cost and latency.

00:01:35.666 --> 00:01:44.130
New word: latency. How long one operation takes from request to answer. Picture it like this: How long you wait for your coffee.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:49.228
Time to live and versioned file names decide freshness.

00:01:49.678 --> 00:01:55.878
Netflix pre-positions popular titles on its Open Connect appliances overnight, before people press play.

00:01:57.228 --> 00:02:03.799
Cache popular content at the edge, shield the origin, and plan freshness with TTLs and versioned names.

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:05.084
Back to the question.

00:02:05.484 --> 00:02:07.794
What is request collapsing at the edge?

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:11.817
Many identical cache misses wait on a single fetch from origin.

00:02:13.017 --> 00:02:13.887
Quick recap.

00:02:14.287 --> 00:02:15.994
Copies live near users.

00:02:16.394 --> 00:02:18.356
TTL controls freshness.

00:02:18.756 --> 00:02:21.856
Shield and collapse requests to protect origin.
